WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.560
Instructor: All right, so next up I wanna talk

00:01.560 --> 00:03.930
a little bit more about how push works

00:03.930 --> 00:07.560
and specifically the relationship between a branch

00:07.560 --> 00:10.800
that we have locally and then a branch on GitHub.

00:10.800 --> 00:12.270
So to begin with,

00:12.270 --> 00:13.980
I'm just gonna make a new repository

00:13.980 --> 00:17.130
to demonstrate this with, a new GitHub repository.

00:17.130 --> 00:21.690
I'm just gonna call this pushme, sure.

00:21.690 --> 00:23.880
I'll make it public, I'll create it

00:23.880 --> 00:28.530
and then I'll just copy this line to add the remote, okay.

00:28.530 --> 00:33.390
And now I'm going to make a new repository locally.

00:33.390 --> 00:35.010
I don't have anything going on here.

00:35.010 --> 00:38.610
I'm going to git init and then I'll just make a file here.

00:38.610 --> 00:43.531
How about candy.txt, sure.

00:43.531 --> 00:45.663
Oh geez, I forgot to touch it.

00:46.650 --> 00:49.570
Okay, so I'm gonna add that empty file

00:50.430 --> 00:54.000
and I'm gonna commit create candy file.

00:54.000 --> 00:56.790
All right, so I'm gonna add this remote.

00:56.790 --> 00:59.130
Right, we've seen this before, git remote add origin,

00:59.130 --> 01:02.340
just the URL for that GitHub repository.

01:02.340 --> 01:04.050
Now when I run this command,

01:04.050 --> 01:09.050
if I wanna push up my master branch, git push origin master.

01:09.060 --> 01:11.160
What this does, as we've already talked about,

01:11.160 --> 01:13.050
is it pushes up my master branch,

01:13.050 --> 01:15.690
whatever commits or on it, up to GitHub.

01:15.690 --> 01:17.730
But what is really important to understand

01:17.730 --> 01:21.900
is that this will actually create a master branch on GitHub

01:21.900 --> 01:23.280
the very first time.

01:23.280 --> 01:25.920
Right, there is no branch on GitHub at the moment

01:25.920 --> 01:29.520
and there is a branch called master locally.

01:29.520 --> 01:32.673
So it's going to create a new master branch on GitHub.

01:33.750 --> 01:35.430
And then I have my local master branch

01:35.430 --> 01:38.160
and they're connected as you can see by this little arrow

01:38.160 --> 01:42.240
it shows me, it just says master pointing to master.

01:42.240 --> 01:44.310
This is something we're going to learn more about

01:44.310 --> 01:47.520
in the next section when we talk about fetching and pulling,

01:47.520 --> 01:49.560
but there's a distinction between the branches

01:49.560 --> 01:51.690
you have locally and the branches on GitHub.

01:51.690 --> 01:54.360
If I refresh here, there we are,

01:54.360 --> 01:57.630
we see the master branch and I see this empty candy file.

01:57.630 --> 02:01.110
Now usually, well a lot of the time,

02:01.110 --> 02:05.220
we want to connect and push our master branch

02:05.220 --> 02:10.200
up to the master branch on GitHub, but we don't have to.

02:10.200 --> 02:11.430
So I wanna be very clear about that.

02:11.430 --> 02:13.230
If I do more work here,

02:13.230 --> 02:18.230
let's just make another file touch candy and Flowers.txt.

02:20.370 --> 02:25.370
I'll add that and I'll commit -m create flowers.

02:27.810 --> 02:30.990
If I do a git push origin master again,

02:30.990 --> 02:34.080
this time it doesn't make a new branch called master

02:34.080 --> 02:35.820
on GitHub, it's already here.

02:35.820 --> 02:38.880
So it's just going to push our master branch

02:38.880 --> 02:40.770
to this master branch on GitHub.

02:40.770 --> 02:42.330
It just assumes that's what we want.

02:42.330 --> 02:43.560
That's what we've told it.

02:43.560 --> 02:48.120
If you just specify this, just a single branch named master,

02:48.120 --> 02:51.300
it's going to, it git is going to take

02:51.300 --> 02:52.530
our local master branch

02:52.530 --> 02:55.980
and push it up to the remote master branch.

02:55.980 --> 02:57.573
So we'll see that.

02:59.040 --> 03:00.630
Hopefully no surprise

03:00.630 --> 03:04.050
and we see this second file, our second commit show up

03:04.050 --> 03:07.920
but we can actually push to different branches.

03:07.920 --> 03:10.560
So this syntax is not that crazy looking,

03:10.560 --> 03:12.750
but right here, it's a little bit intimidating

03:12.750 --> 03:16.020
when I write it out with the official definition.

03:16.020 --> 03:20.280
Basically if we write git push and then a remote like origin

03:20.280 --> 03:23.640
and then I specify a local branch, a colon,

03:23.640 --> 03:26.190
and a remote branch, this would enable me to push

03:26.190 --> 03:30.240
a local branch to a remote branch of a different name.

03:30.240 --> 03:33.570
So take the local pancake branch,

03:33.570 --> 03:35.790
that one goes first local on my machine

03:35.790 --> 03:39.780
and push it to the origins branch called Waffle.

03:39.780 --> 03:42.900
So I'm gonna demonstrate this, it's nowhere near as common

03:42.900 --> 03:46.200
but I do wanna make it clear that there's a distinction.

03:46.200 --> 03:47.640
They don't have to be the same,

03:47.640 --> 03:50.172
you don't have to push master up to master

03:50.172 --> 03:52.020
even though that's the default behavior

03:52.020 --> 03:54.870
if you just run git push origin master.

03:54.870 --> 03:56.523
So why don't I make a new branch?

03:57.480 --> 03:58.560
What should this one be called?

03:58.560 --> 04:00.600
How about cats, okay.

04:00.600 --> 04:02.430
So I have this new branch called cats

04:02.430 --> 04:05.070
and I'm gonna make some changes on it.

04:05.070 --> 04:10.070
Like just add, I guess we'll just make a cats file.

04:10.170 --> 04:12.270
I know this is quite the boring repository.

04:12.270 --> 04:13.890
Let's add cats.

04:13.890 --> 04:15.330
I'm on this branch, right?

04:15.330 --> 04:18.930
So create cat's file, I'm on the cat's branch.

04:18.930 --> 04:22.050
If I do a git push origin cats,

04:22.050 --> 04:25.620
that will push up my local cats branch to a branch

04:25.620 --> 04:27.390
on origin that doesn't exist yet,

04:27.390 --> 04:29.970
it will create it, called cats.

04:29.970 --> 04:32.970
But if I instead, I don't want that to happen,

04:32.970 --> 04:35.250
which I'll just do it first, so you see that,

04:35.250 --> 04:38.820
just more practice, right, the master branch is unchanged.

04:38.820 --> 04:42.360
We don't see cats, but if I go to the cats branch,

04:42.360 --> 04:45.870
can toggle them here, we see the Cat's file.

04:45.870 --> 04:50.580
Anyway, if I instead want to get that work on master,

04:50.580 --> 04:53.160
not a normal thing to do, but I'm just gonna show this.

04:53.160 --> 04:57.713
If I do git push origin and then my local branch, cats,

04:58.560 --> 05:01.350
and then the destination branch on the remote,

05:01.350 --> 05:03.960
this is the syntax, local branch that we have

05:03.960 --> 05:05.370
on our machine colon,

05:05.370 --> 05:07.890
and then the name of a branch on GitHub,

05:07.890 --> 05:11.640
so master or if we had some other branch on GitHub, sure.

05:11.640 --> 05:14.250
This tells gits to take our local cats branch

05:14.250 --> 05:16.170
and push it up to the origin master branch.

05:16.170 --> 05:17.013
Let's try it.

05:19.260 --> 05:21.240
Okay, and we see this diagram,

05:21.240 --> 05:23.550
it's not a diagram really, but we see this,

05:23.550 --> 05:26.640
it says or it tells us it pushed the cats branch

05:26.640 --> 05:29.040
to the master branch this time.

05:29.040 --> 05:30.120
So if I refresh now,

05:30.120 --> 05:33.750
on the master branch on GitHub, we see that cats file.

05:33.750 --> 05:35.820
Even though we didn't push the master branch

05:35.820 --> 05:39.210
from our machine up, we pushed the cats branch

05:39.210 --> 05:42.810
but we pushed it to the GitHub master branch.

05:42.810 --> 05:44.820
So I know that's kind of confusing,

05:44.820 --> 05:48.120
and again, not at all common, but you can do it,

05:48.120 --> 05:50.190
and I just showed it to you to make it clear

05:50.190 --> 05:52.410
that there's a distinction between the branch

05:52.410 --> 05:54.540
on your machine and the branch on GitHub

05:54.540 --> 05:56.340
and when we normally do things

05:56.340 --> 05:58.620
like this git push origin master.

05:58.620 --> 06:00.360
Yes, we're pushing the master branch

06:00.360 --> 06:03.210
from our machine to the master branch on GitHub.

06:03.210 --> 06:05.310
Normally we want them to be the same

06:05.310 --> 06:07.593
but they don't have to be, all right.
